When was that?  1970?  1971?  Haven't any of the VMS fanatics here even
looked at their competition lately?

Let's take a look at one that just showed up on one of the professor's
desks.  OpenSolaris.

Insert CD
Boot
Asks what keyboard you want (40 som to choose from) if in the US, hit return.
Asks what language to use for the install (about a dozen to choose from)
  if in the US, hit return.
Read License on screen.
Click CLOSE.
4 icons on desktop.
Double click "Install OpenSolaris".
Read WELCOME SCREEN
Click NEXT
Select DISK to use. 
We will assume a novice with no real knowledge of disk geometery.
First Option "Use Entire Disk"
Click NEXT
Set Timezone.  All done with GUI.
Click NEXT
Select Locale.  About a dozen languages to choose from.  Even a novice
should figure this one out.
Click NEXT

Now, the biggest screen you have to fill in!!
Enter a root password.
Create a new user and give him a password.
Name the computer or just accept the default.
Click NEXT
Verify your selections.  Happy?
Click INSTALL
Go get cup of Coffee
Come back.
Click REBOOT.

System completely installed including the GUI.

Tell me again how hard Unix is compared to VMS?

bill
 

